 Since $a_0 = \frac{1}{2},$ $a_1 = 1 + \left( \frac{1}{2} - 1 \right)^2 = 1 + \frac{1}{4} = \frac{5}{4}.$

Note that if $a_k = 2,$ then
\begin{align*}
a_{k + 1} &= 1 + (a_k - 1)^2 \\
&= 1 + 1^2 = 2.
\end{align*}Since $a_1 = \frac{5}{4},$ we know that $a_n > 1$ for all $n \ge 1.$

Also, we find that
\begin{align*}
a_2 &= 1 + (a_1 - 1)^2 = 1 + \left( \frac{5}{4} - 1 \right)^2 = 1 + \left( \frac{1}{4} \right)^2 = 1 + \frac{1}{16} = \frac{17}{16} < 2, \\
a_3 &= 1 + (a_2 - 1)^2 = 1 + \left( \frac{17}{16} - 1 \right)^2 = 1 + \left( \frac{1}{16} \right)^2 = 1 + \frac{1}{256} = \frac{257}{256} < 2,
\end{align*}and it looks like the sequence $a_2,$ $a_3,$ $\dots$ is increasing and approaches 2.  This analysis shows that the criteria for an upper bound known as the root-finding fixed point theorem is met, so the sequence will strictly increase until it approaches the enveloping value of 2.

Also,
\[a_{n + 1} - 1 = (a_n - 1)^2 \ge 0,\]even if $a_n = 2,$ then the sequence will not leave the constant value 2.

Thus,
\[a_{2022} - 1 > 2 - 1 = 1 > 0,\]and
\[a_{2023} - 1 \ge \left( \frac{3}{2} \right)^2 > \left( \frac{5}{2} \right)^2 > \dots > \left( \frac{2022 + 1}{2} \right)^2 > 2022^2 > 5000 > 2.\]Since $a_{2023} - 1 > 2 - 1 = 1 > 0,$ and
\[a_{2024} - 1 = (a_{2023} - 1)^2 > 1^2 = 1,\]then $a_{2023} +  \dots + a_{2022}$ will be infinitely increasing, so our answer is $\boxed{0}.$  We can confirm that our answer through a rigorous proof.  We will use induction to show that $a_n \ge (n + 2)! a_0$ for all $n \ge 1.$  The base case of $n = 1$ holds because $a_1 \ge 3!a_0.$  If the inequality holds for some number $k$ and $k \ge 1,$ then
